---
description: >
  Three layers of drift in this spec. (1) Active fixed-`brain`-key drift —
  every outcome and strategy paragraph names the obsidian MCP key as the
  shipped name, but `.aide/handoff.aide` settles the key at `brain`.
  (2) Active post-move drift — stale scope path and "init orchestrator"
  references inherited from the previous parent identity. (3) Resolved —
  prior cycle's seed-semantic-idempotency review.
intent: >
  Two active drifts: the fixed-`brain`-key invariant from `.aide/handoff.aide`
  has not been applied to this spec's outcomes (which still name
  `obsidian` as the MCP key prescribed), and the post-move identity
  drift naming `src/tools/init/provisionBrain` and "the init
  orchestrator's apply layer" persists. The handoff explicitly names this
  module as the migration surface for the legacy `obsidian` key — but
  the spec describes a world where `obsidian` is the canonical key, not
  the legacy one being migrated away from.
misalignment:
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

### Active (alignment pass 2026-04-25 — root revision: brain.aide path move + launcher rename + schema collapse)

The root `.aide/intent.aide` was rewritten to move brain.aide to `.aide/config/brain.aide`, change the default launcher to `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`, collapse the frontmatter schema to exactly two fields (`name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`), rename `connector` to `name`, and remove `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`, and the `invalid-path` boot state. This spec authored the canonical scaffold template and is the load-bearing producer of the post-handoff schema; nearly every paragraph drifts.

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` description: "Plans vault directory scaffolding... brain config file scaffolding..."
  Per the new root, brain config scaffolding writes to `.aide/config/brain.aide`, NOT `.aide/brain.aide`. The description should name the new path explicitly because the directory itself (`.aide/config/`) carries the user-owned scaffold-once contract.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (`.aide/config/` is the canonical home of user-owned configuration; files inside are scaffolded once at `aide_init` if absent, then belong to the user forever).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` intent paragraph: "...primary output is the scaffolded `.aide/brain.aide`..."
  Stale path. The canonical location is `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The paragraph also names `parseBrainAide` as the derivation source — that's still correct in shape, but the parser's input file path moves with the brain.aide path.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (canonical and only source of truth at `.aide/config/brain.aide`).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired ("The brain config step plans creation of `.aide/brain.aide`..."): pins the file path to the legacy location.
  Should read `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The "category: brain" label is correct for the install service plan.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (path move).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired ("the canonical Obsidian brain.aide bytes from obsidianBrainAideTemplate"): names the template helper as `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` and pins the launcher to "Obsidian template's launcher shape" with "command: cmd /c npx ..." and "command: npx ..." platform-branching examples.
  Under the new root the default launcher is `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`, not `obsidian-mcp`. Either the template helper renames to reflect the new launcher, or the launcher choice becomes a user-owned decision the template no longer pins. The outcome that the template ships "the canonical Obsidian brain.aide bytes" needs to either name the new launcher invocation or reframe what "canonical Obsidian default" means under the new setup.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired ("scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` pre-filled with a canonical Obsidian configuration that launches via `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`").
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.undesired ("Scaffolding a brain.aide whose `connector` value the package later branches on"): names the frontmatter field as `connector`.
  The field has been renamed to `name`. The undesired-outcome's structural rule (the package never branches on it) is unchanged and load-bearing, but the field name is stale. A scaffolded brain.aide with `connector:` in its frontmatter would now fail validation against the parser, which expects `name:`.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (`name` is the only label field; the package never branches on it).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Good examples (Step 1 brain config example block): renders the canonical template content with frontmatter fields `connector: obsidian`, `rootPath: D:/notes/my-vault`, `entryFile: CLAUDE.md`, `mcpServerConfig` referencing `obsidian-mcp` and `${rootPath}`, and a `tools:` block with `read:` / `search:` keys.
  Every one of those fields is removed or renamed in the new root: `connector` → `name`, `rootPath` removed (path inlined into args), `entryFile` removed, `tools` removed entirely. The new canonical template should show only `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` with `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` and the path inlined byte-for-byte. The `${rootPath}` placeholder still works for advanced users but the default scaffold should not exercise it.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (frontmatter contains exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` — no `connector`, no `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools`); ("The default scaffolded `brain.aide` makes no use of substitution: `mcpServerConfig.args` carries the path inline, byte-for-byte under user ownership"); ("scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` pre-filled with a canonical Obsidian configuration that launches via `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`").
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Good examples (Step 5 — MCP config (brain)): "Prescription derived from the Step 1 content bytes: parseBrainAide parses the template string, interpolateArgs substitutes `${rootPath}`..."
  The interpolation surface still exists for advanced users but the default scaffold no longer exercises `${rootPath}` (the path is byte-inline). The example should show the default-scaffold path: parser reads the file, sync writes `mcpServerConfig.args` through unchanged. Substitution becomes the opt-in path for advanced users who DRY a value across positions.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (default scaffold makes no use of substitution; the parser still supports `${...}` for advanced users).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Bad examples ("A provisionBrain that hardcodes the Windows launcher shape in index.ts"): the inline counter-example shows `obsidian-mcp` as the launcher arg.
  The shape of the bad example is still load-bearing (don't duplicate the launcher bytes outside the template), but the launcher string should be `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (default launcher).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Bad examples ("A brain.aide scaffolded with intent-spec frontmatter fields"): the counter-example block still includes `connector: obsidian` in the intended-non-conflicting fields after the rejected intent-spec fields.
  Should be `name: obsidian` to reflect the rename. The structural lesson (no scope/intent/outcomes in brain.aide) is unchanged.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (rename `connector` → `name`).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Strategy ("`obsidianBrainAideTemplate` lives in its own helper subfolder"): the template helper's identity is bound to the legacy `obsidian-mcp` launcher. With the launcher rename, either the helper is renamed or its responsibilities shift (it now ships the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` default).
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (default launcher).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Strategy ("Signature takes `projectRoot`, not just `brainPath` and `mcpConfigPath`"): the signature is still correct but the rationale ("derive the brain.aide path at `.aide/brain.aide`") names the legacy path. With brain.aide moving to `.aide/config/brain.aide`, the path-derivation logic changes.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (path move).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired (seed-semantic idempotency for the brain config file): the seed-semantic invariant is correct in spirit and even reinforced by the new root's directory-level rule (`.aide/config/` is scaffolded once, owned by user forever, never overwritten by init or upgrade). The spec should restate the rule in directory-boundary terms — the install/upgrade tooling reads the path as the ownership signal, not a per-file allowlist — so future readers understand the invariant is now structural, not per-file.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired (`.aide/config/` boundary as ownership signal, no per-file allowlist).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

### Active (alignment pass — fixed-`brain`-key drift)

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` frontmatter description: "...and Obsidian MCP wiring as InitStep items"
  Under the fixed-key settlement (`.aide/handoff.aide`: "The brain MCP key is fixed at `brain`"), this helper now plans wiring under the `brain` key, not the `obsidian` key. The implementation has been migrated (handoff: "`buildObsidianMcpStep` renamed to `buildBrainMcpStep`"); the spec description still names the old key.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled) — fixed `brain` key + the handoff's "Done in this session" entry naming `provisionBrain` as the migration surface.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired ("The Obsidian MCP server prescription is planned with the correct platform-aware command... and reports 'exists' when the obsidian key is already present in the MCP config"): names the prescribed key as `obsidian`. The new contract: the prescription writes under the fixed `brain` key. The "already present" check now runs against `mcpServers["brain"]`, not `mcpServers["obsidian"]`. The legacy `obsidian` key is the one being migrated away from, not the one being prescribed.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled) -> "Migration path: rename `obsidian` -> `brain` at install time" — this spec is the migration surface, not the canonical-key prescriber.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Strategy ("Four planning steps, not two"): "...the playbook hub template, vault CLAUDE.md, and MCP prescription. Each is its own InitStep with independent idempotency..."
  The MCP-prescription step is correct in structure but the strategy paragraph in the surrounding paragraphs ("MCP prescription uses canonical-template idempotency... if the on-disk command string drifts from the platform-aware prescription...") still references `obsidian` as the key the byte-comparison runs against. Per the handoff, comparison runs against the `brain` key with three migration branches: cold install -> would-create, legacy `obsidian`-only -> would-overwrite with key `brain`, both keys present -> would-overwrite with key `brain`.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` Done-this-session — "Three migration branches: cold install -> `would-create`, legacy `obsidian`-only -> `would-overwrite` with key `brain`, both keys present -> `would-overwrite` with key `brain`."
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Strategy ("Two different idempotency semantics coexist..."): "The Obsidian MCP prescription, by contrast, uses canonical-template idempotency: it is a config value the tool owns, so byte-identity is the right signal — if the on-disk command string drifts from the platform-aware prescription, the step reports 'would-overwrite' because the tool is the source of truth for that entry."
  The two-idempotency-mode distinction is correct and load-bearing, but the paragraph names the prescription as "the Obsidian MCP prescription." Should read "the brain MCP prescription" — the prescription is keyed `brain` regardless of which backend's command/args it carries. The paragraph also pre-dates the migration-branches addition: it does not mention the legacy-`obsidian`-key migration semantics at all, even though the handoff names this exact module as where that migration runs.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled) — fixed `brain` key + migration semantics.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Good examples (Step 4): "Step 4 — Obsidian MCP. Status: would-create. Category: mcp. Prescription contains the server key and platform-aware command entry."
  The example label "Obsidian MCP" should read "Brain MCP" (or just "MCP"). The prescription's entry value is obsidian-shaped, but the step's identity in the install plan is "the brain entry."
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Good examples (re-running prose): "The MCP prescription sees the obsidian key already present in the MCP config..."
  Should read "the brain key already present." On re-run, the migration is already complete; the canonical key is `brain`.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Strategy and Bad examples: no mention of the three migration branches the implementation now ships (cold install, legacy-`obsidian`-only, both keys present). The handoff explicitly names this module as the one-place migration surface; the spec should describe the migration semantics or at least reference them, otherwise downstream agents reading the spec will not know that migration is part of this helper's job.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` Done-this-session — "Migration path: rename `obsidian` -> `brain` at install time. `provisionBrain` emits a `would-overwrite` step for legacy installs during their next `/aide:upgrade` or `/aide:init`."
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` Context paragraph: "...the playbook hub and vault CLAUDE.md must also be present so agents can navigate the vault and discover conventions..." — the surrounding context describes the seeded vault `CLAUDE.md` template but does not flag that the seeded template's prose currently emits `mcp__obsidian__*` tool names, which are stale under the fixed-`brain`-key invariant. Per the handoff Remaining-work entry "`provisionBrain` modernization (deferred)," this is acknowledged as still-pending work — but the spec's outcomes do not commit to the post-handoff prose. Either the spec needs to flag the deferred modernization, or the modernization needs to land before the spec is realigned.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` Remaining-work -> "`provisionBrain` modernization (deferred). Currently writes an Obsidian-shaped vault `CLAUDE.md` template with `mcp__obsidian__*` references baked in. Now stale (the prose should reference `mcp__brain__*` since the MCP key is fixed at `brain`)."
  Misalignment: spec-gap

